{
  "dom_id": "step-q250840",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given that $\\frac{x}{4}$, $x$, and $64$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=\\frac{x}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=x,\\,{a}_{3}=64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{(\\frac{x}{4})}=x\\cdot \\frac{4}{x}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{64}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{64}{x} \\\\ 4x & =64 \\\\ x & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
